[ti:The Westhaven Express]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.22]Lesson 37 | 第37课
[00:02.10]The Westhaven Express | 开往威斯特海温的快车
[00:08.86]What was the mistake the author made? | 作者犯了个什么错误？
[00:14.04]We have learnt to expect that trains will be punctual. | 我们已经习惯于相信火车会准点。
[00:18.46]After years of conditioning, most of us have developed an unshakable faith in railway timetables. | 经过多年的适应，大多数人对铁路时刻表产生了一种不可动摇的信任。
[00:26.14]Ships may be delayed by storms; | 轮船可能因风暴误期，
[00:28.93]flights may be cancelled because of bad weather; but trains must be on time. | 飞机可能因恶劣天气停飞，但火车必须准点。
[00:35.90]Only an exceptionally heavy snow fall might temporarily dislocate railway services. | 只有非同寻常的大雪才可能暂时打乱铁路运行。
[00:43.33]It is all too easy to blame the railway authorities when something does go wrong. | 一旦出了问题，人们很容易归咎于铁路当局。
[00:49.57]The truth is that when mistakes occur, they are more likely to be ours than theirs. | 事实上，出错的很可能是我们自己，而不是他们。
[00:56.76]After consulting my railway timetable, I noted with satisfaction that there was an express train to Westhaven. | 我查了查列车时刻表，满意地获悉有一班开往威斯特海温的快车。
[01:06.00]It went direct from my local station and the journey lasted a mere hour and seventeen minutes. | 这趟车从我家附近的车站直达目的地，全程仅需1小时17分钟。
[01:13.55]When I boarded the train, I could not help noticing that a great many local people got on as well. | 上车后，我不禁注意到许多本地人也上了车。
[01:21.11]At the time, this did not strike me as odd. | 当时我并不觉得这有什么奇怪。
[01:24.97]I reflected that there must be a great many people besides myself who wished to take advantage of this excellent service. | 我心想，除了我之外，想利用这班优质服务的人一定很多。
[01:33.31]Neither was I surprised when the train stopped at Widley, a tiny station a few miles along the line. | 火车停在沿线的一个小站威德里时，我也没有感到奇怪。
[01:41.74]Even a mighty express train can be held up by signals. | 再快的快车也可能被信号拦住。
[01:45.94]But when the train dawdled at station after station, I began to wonder. | 但是当火车在一个又一个车站慢慢吞吞地停靠时，我开始纳闷了。
[01:52.11]It suddenly dawned on me that this express was not roaring down the line at ninety miles an hour, but barely chugging along at thirty. | 我突然明白，这趟“快车”并不是以每小时90英里的速度呼啸前行，而是以每小时30英里的速度噗噗前进。
[02:02.90]One hour and seventeen minutes passed and we had not even covered half the distance. | 1小时17分钟过去了，我们连一半的路程都没走完。
[02:09.85]I asked a passenger if this was the Westhaven Express, but he had not even heard of it. | 我问一位乘客这是不是开往威斯特海温的快车，可他连听都没听说过。
[02:16.56]I determined to lodge a complaint as soon as we arrived. | 我决定一到站就提出投诉。
[02:21.06]Two hours later, I was talking angrily to the station master at Westhaven. | 两小时后，我怒气冲冲地对威斯特海温的站长说话。
[02:27.30]When he denied the train's existence, I borrowed his copy of the timetable. | 当站长否认这趟车的存在时，我借来了他的时刻表。
[02:33.44]There was a note of triumph in my voice when I told him that it was there in black and white. | 我带着胜利的口吻告诉他，这趟车白纸黑字印在时刻表上。
[02:40.49]Glancing at it briefly, he told me to look again. | 他草草看了一眼，让我再看一遍。
[02:45.15]A tiny asterisk conducted me to a footnote at the bottom of the page. | 一个小小的星号把我引向页脚的一行脚注。
[02:50.96]It said: 'This service has been suspended.' | 上面写着：“此趟班车暂停运行。”
